Lec 29
 
\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)} = \text{rational function}
 
 
 
Case 1: \boldsymbol{\text{degree } P < \text{degree } Q}
 
Cover-up method
 
Example 1:
\frac{4x-1}{(x-1)(x+2)} = \frac{A}{x-1} + \frac{B}{x+2}
 
Example 2:
\frac{x^2+2}{(x-1)^2(x+2)} = \frac{A}{(x-1)} + \frac{B}{(x-1)^2} + \frac{C}{(x+2)}
 
Example 3:
\frac{x^2}{(x-1)(x^2+1)} = \frac{A}{(x-1)} + \frac{Bx+C}{(x^2+1)}
 
 
 
Case 2: \boldsymbol{\text{degree } P \ge \text{degree } Q}
 
This is an improper fraction → cannot use cover-up method directly.
 
Example:
\frac{x^3}{(x-1)(x+2)} = \frac{x^3}{x^2+x-2}
We use long division first:
 
x - 1
____________
x²+x-2 | x³
x³ + x² - 2x
-------------
-x² + 2x
-x² - x + 2
-------------
3x - 2
 
So:
\frac{x^3}{x^2+x-2} = \underbrace{x-1}_{\text{quotient}} + \underbrace{\frac{3x-2}{x^2+x-2}}_{\text{remainder (proper fraction)}}




