Lec 34/35: L’Hôpital’s Rule
 
Derivation (where f(a)=g(a)=0)
 

\lim_{x\to a}\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}
= \lim_{x\to a}\frac{f(x)/(x-a)}{g(x)/(x-a)}
= \frac{\lim_{x\to a}\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}}{\lim_{x\to a}\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}}
= \frac{f'(a)}{g'(a)} \quad (\text{works if }g'(a)\neq0)

 
L’Hôpital’s Rule (VERSION 1)
 

\lim_{x\to a}\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}
= \lim_{x\to a}\frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)}

Provided:
 
- f(a)=g(a)=0
​
- and the right-hand limit exists.
 
Other cases allowed:
 
- a=\pm\infty
​
- f(a),g(a)\to\infty (the \frac{\infty}{\infty} case)
​
- R.H.S exists or \to\pm\infty
​
- Applied to \frac{f(a)}{g(a)} \to \frac{0}{0} or \frac{\infty}{\infty}
 
Additional Result
 

\lim_{x\to0^+}x^x=1


