Lec 37: Infinite Series
 
Examples

\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^2} \quad \longleftrightarrow \quad \int_{1}^{\infty} \frac{dx}{x^2} \quad \text{converges}


\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^p} \quad \longleftrightarrow \quad \int_{1}^{\infty} \frac{dx}{x^p} \quad \text{converges if } p>1

(Note: \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{1}{n^2} = \frac{\pi^2}{6})
 
 
 
Integral Comparison Test
 
If f(x) is decreasing and f(x) > 0, then:

\left| \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} f(n) - \int_{1}^{\infty} f(x) dx \right| < f(1)

 
- \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} f(n) and \int_{1}^{\infty} f(x) dx converge or diverge together.
 
 
 
Limit Comparison Test
 
If f(n) \sim g(n) (meaning \frac{f(n)}{g(n)} \to 1 as n \to \infty)
and g(n) > 0,
then \sum f(n) and \sum g(n)
either both converge or both diverge.



Lec 38: Power Series
 
Geometric Series Example

1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + \dots = \frac{1}{1-x} \quad |x| < 1 \quad [\text{converges}]

 
Taylor’s Formula

f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!} x^n

 
- 0! = 1
​
- Coefficient formula: a_n = \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}
 
 
 
General Power Series
 

a_0 + a_1 x + a_2 x^2 + a_3 x^3 + \dots = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n x^n

 
Radius of Convergence (R)
 
- For |x| < R: the series converges
​
- Interval of convergence: (-R, R)
​
- |a_n x^n| \to 0 as n \to \infty
​
- For |x| > R: the series diverges
​
- |a_n x^n| \nrightarrow 0 as n \to \infty
 
 
 
Lec 39: Error Function (erf)
 
Definition

\text{erf}(x) = \frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}} \int_{0}^{x} e^{-t^2} dt

 
- Property: \lim_{x \to \infty} \text{erf}(x) = 1